Returning to the conditions of corollary 1, b), in characteristic $p>0$, working with the prime-to-$p$ fundamental groups, one finds that the $a$ in 1°) is a point \inserted{of $G(K)$} of order a power of $p$. If $G$ is a torus (for this it suffices that the ``fibres'' in the elementary variety $Y$ be of genus 0) one still concludes that $a=0$, that is $f=g$. In the general case, the arithmetic proof proves \inserted{(\unsure{we shall see|we shall do})} this : if $x\in X$, \deleted{and} \inserted{whence} $f(x)\inserted{=P}$, $g(x)=Q$ in $Y$, not only is $Q-P$ a $p$-torsion element of the generalized Jacobian $G_Y$ of $Y$, for every finite Galois \inserted{étale} covering $Y'$ of $X$, \inserted{of degree prime to $p$,} pointed by a $P'$ above $P$, there exists $Q'$ above $Q$ such that $Q'-P'$ is a \unsure{?} $p$-torsion element of $G_{Y'}$.

\deleted{Passing to the coverings by \inserted{abelian coverings, \unsure{multiples},} isogenies ${}_nG$ ($n$ prime to \unsure{$G$|$p$}), there exists a $Q_n\in G$ such that $\unsure{{}_n nQ_n|nQ_n}=Q$, and such that $Q_n$}
It could be that one can deduce $P=Q$ from this.

\margin{this shows that theorem 2 remains true, over a base field of characteristic $p>0$, working with the prime-to-$p$ fundamental groups -- for there in the reduction to a geometric statement, \unsure{one sees that up to étale maps}, one can suppose that $Y$ is a \uline{product} of curves of genus zero. In this case moreover, one is reduced to the case where $Y$ is $=$ such a curve,....}
